#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564591} :activity/id #uuid "8c4631f5-ce38-50ca-8db4-4b2dd080d0df" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050205 :topic/id #uuid "36128875-8025-5e37-8397-54d8247d47c9" :topic/math-academy-id 3131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d8c257-9208-59a3-b8ef-e84226b5d7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400765} {:answer/id #uuid "c56b5625-8f3f-5546-bd45-82c8dcf4f2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$25$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400766} {:answer/id #uuid "9f361d5f-db40-531a-8f20-5389f05b015d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{7}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400767} {:answer/id #uuid "c82a5223-3bd0-5bad-9e6b-edd5899707f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$11$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400768} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a6a4f9-ba6e-5a5b-9c4a-a88207909379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400765} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dac3c89-e776-5135-b836-fafd4c581933" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "925e17f0-1f91-564c-b150-3d5633a84c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139546" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6df3639cb222756221803446ebc735cf1e7cbcf4a922ed7fce835c0c44fb9aa1.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=5={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=3={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "410080ab-5733-5c31-94d9-fda79a0bc8c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5df5ba-75f0-5bfc-a30e-6793337660ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503195} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6dfca0-cb60-5c5b-ae49-7cd685619f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503239} {:answer/id #uuid "87db152d-bf36-549b-bb17-dd5daccc80b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503259} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b73526-0075-5162-b576-f6193e008526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503320} {:answer/id #uuid "f92b14f4-9494-5bbe-b406-714b16837483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503239} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e35f8d5-2126-585d-9e2b-f8a2c67ed434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2c3caea-6032-5d20-9fea-52c78472efb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139538" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={8}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={8}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8.$"} :step/id #uuid "45611da9-d3af-5cc7-b007-af7ba19a6423" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a429996-164b-54fc-9946-577431de1ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266657} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3a6a58-573b-5732-bf74-6c0a1ac7d701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266849} {:answer/id #uuid "62be0fb0-0dcb-57b3-9f91-1d3ab410a49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267012} {:answer/id #uuid "afc186cf-aab9-589d-af44-efe97c49758b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267725} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7691e1-a3bb-53c8-9a1f-15a33a8deed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266657} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a4b6387-6d51-5a91-bc9a-e1d31b9ff78c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed90c3b2-491c-5a1e-a27e-a0b50d59ad00" :question/math-academy-id "q-139535" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={9}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={9}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=9.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9.$"} :step/id #uuid "7935da3a-469b-5bce-9e33-166b16dfa9be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4adfe18a-fa58-5f98-9ac3-e41976c7232c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159377} {:answer/id #uuid "57323d56-0255-5884-8e73-45ea1c7fcac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$16$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159378} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9066f0-4a14-571e-b7a0-ef918b097a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159379} {:answer/id #uuid "38f9c426-dab0-57b7-aa76-6f687589b344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4{v}_{1}$ and $2{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564065} {:answer/id #uuid "585850a0-2e98-5454-a5ce-c2c74b29b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186159379} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddea8248-482b-5038-b9ed-6aad287810e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba175b74-8169-5d32-9cc8-7cf8e202ba3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139420" :question/problem "![](images/93/931f7cc1fe4cc6a45c1f586bb017bb3eda8f7745aa3973c13f08aa8521c39400.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=4={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=2={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "ec8da15c-64b8-543b-87b6-4d2e08009505" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252f6f3b-5a10-5189-8e0c-406c29122f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{A}^{T}{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266477} {:answer/id #uuid "77ec066c-ebeb-56f0-86e7-24ebc13114e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267194} {:answer/id #uuid "afcf1cd5-5c2b-558f-b5b8-acc6c19c0e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267727} {:answer/id #uuid "c409ba11-4a9d-56ac-922a-569a66b0dde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267894} {:answer/id #uuid "cf182881-986b-5994-95c4-d3b02929dcab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267996}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267894} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cec0f57-03b7-5e8d-a4e5-e4bf91fb6e3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f621452-f60c-5e1d-b4df-620aa05fd780" :question/math-academy-id "q-139555" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d1cefc0c789518d21279c5c49a18f80220a2aad80973f0e84fda0ddaf25493b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $100$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=10={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={10}^{2}=100,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=8={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={8}^{2}=64.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=100$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "fdfac010-d775-5eb5-8960-f4712e637036"}] :activity/title "The Singular Values of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563963}]}
